<h2>Seventeen suits, one question</h2>
<p>The batch — trademark infringement, copyright, trade dress and passing off claims by companies ranging from liquor and FMCG manufacturers to restaurants and ceramics brands — shared a single jurisdictional foundation. Each plaintiff said Delhi had territorial jurisdiction because the defendant&rsquo;s website or online marketplace listing was accessible in Delhi, because transactions could in theory be concluded on it, or because an order had in fact been placed for delivery at a Delhi address. In the lead suit, a Mumbai-based beverages company alleged that a Madhya Pradesh distiller&rsquo;s vodka infringed its mark and trade dress; the Delhi connection rested on website accessibility, an asserted commercial presence and a GST registration reflecting a Delhi address — while the defendant&rsquo;s own product material indicated sale only in Madhya Pradesh.</p>
<p>The court was blunt about what was at stake. A suit with no material connection to Delhi should not be entertained here: institutional discipline, the court said, requires it to refrain. If accessibility were enough, every seller with a webpage would be suable everywhere, and territorial jurisdiction — the rule that keeps litigation near the dispute and the defendant — would mean nothing.</p>

<h2>The legal architecture the judgment reaffirms</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>&ldquo;Carries on business&rdquo;</h3><p>Under the settled Supreme Court test, carrying on business at a place requires an interest in the business there, a voice in its conduct, a share in its gains or losses, and control — not the mere fact that goods are sold or deliverable there, including through a &ldquo;virtual store&rdquo;.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Composite suits</h3><p>Where infringement and passing off are combined, jurisdiction must be made out for each relief separately. The special venue provisions of the Trade Marks Act and Copyright Act do not carry the passing off claim with them.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Targeting, not access</h3><p>For website-based jurisdiction, the operative question is whether the defendant purposefully targeted customers within the forum and concluded genuine commercial transactions there — the standard long associated with the Division Bench&rsquo;s trap-transaction jurisprudence.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Statutory venue discipline</h3><p>Where a corporation has a principal office and sub-offices, a sub-office counts only if the cause of action arises there. The convenience-driven expansion of forum choice through online listings was expressly rejected.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The earlier &ldquo;virtual shop&rdquo; reasoning confined</h2>
<p>The most consequential doctrinal move is the court&rsquo;s treatment of the 2014 Division Bench view that an interactive website amounts to a shop wherever the buyer sits. After tracing the Supreme Court&rsquo;s subsequent decisions on the special venue provisions and the principal-office framework, the single judge concluded that the earlier reasoning departs from the Supreme Court&rsquo;s test and no longer governs the issue — it stands confined to its own facts. The court also noted that a related jurisdictional question is pending before a larger bench, but proceeded to decide, observing that a pending reference does not stay other proceedings and that there cannot be a vacuum in the law.</p>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>The plaintiffs&rsquo; theory</h3><p>Website opens in Delhi; orders can be placed from Delhi; our representative received delivery in Delhi; the GST portal shows a Delhi address — therefore Delhi courts have jurisdiction.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>The court&rsquo;s answer</h3><p>Accessibility proves nothing about where business is carried on; an arranged purchase cannot manufacture a connection; a tax registration is not the CPC test; and the plaintiff has no inherent right to a forum of its preference.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The outcome and its mechanics</h2>
<p>Finding territorial jurisdiction not established, the court returned the plaints under Order VII Rule 10 read with Section 151 CPC, leaving the plaintiffs to the procedure under Order VII Rule 10A, and disposed of the pending applications. Return is not rejection: the claims remain alive and can be presented before the courts within whose jurisdiction the defendants reside or the causes of action genuinely arose. What the plaintiffs lose is the chosen forum — and with it, often, the practical pressure that litigating in a distant city places on a smaller defendant, a dynamic the judgment expressly flagged when it warned that expansive online-accessibility jurisdiction invites harassment and forum shopping.</p>

<h2>What this means for commercial plaintiffs and defendants</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plead jurisdiction as fact, not formula.</strong> Recitals that the defendant&rsquo;s website is &ldquo;accessible in Delhi&rdquo; will attract Order VII Rule 10 scrutiny. Plead concrete, defendant-initiated commercial activity within the forum.</li>
  <li><strong>Audit trap purchases.</strong> If a test purchase is the backbone of jurisdiction, be prepared to show that it reflects the defendant&rsquo;s real course of trade into the forum rather than an engineered transaction.</li>
  <li><strong>Defendants should raise the objection early.</strong> A return of plaint application, taken at the threshold, can relocate the entire litigation before costs mount — and in commercial suits the timelines make early objections doubly valuable.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch the larger bench.</strong> With a reference pending on the wider question, parties structuring e-commerce operations should track how the targeting standard is finally settled.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> For businesses selling online across India, this judgment is a planning document. Where you can be sued is now tied to where you purposefully direct trade — pricing pages, delivery coverage, dealer networks and marketplace settings are jurisdictional evidence. Keep records that show which states you actually serve, and review forum-selection and dispute-resolution clauses in distribution contracts so that the forum question is answered by drafting rather than by litigation.</p>
</div>
